    "updated_at": "2022-12-25T17:47:33Z",
    "body": "> As a workaround, you could try configuring `sudo` to preserve `SSH_CONNECTION`, `SSH_CLIENT` and `SSH_TTY`.\r\n\r\nWell, `sudo -E su` preserves the environment and does work. As most people I'm using `sudo -i` and if I use `sudo command` I don't have a prompt anyway. It still usable, just would be nice to have the most common use-case covered.",
